(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-2-exam-1-pd2-exam-1-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"PD2 Exam 1 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which causes produce CENTRAL vertigo?","choices":["Otitis media, otitis externa and Eustachian tube dysfunction","Brainstem lesion","Benign positional vertigo, Ménière disease and labyrinthitis","Loop diuretics, aminoglycosides, salicylates and alcohol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — all vascular, demyelinating or structural problems above the inner ear. Atherosclerosis, multiple sclerosis, vertebrobasilar migraine and transient ischaemic attack. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"The memory aid \"bugs, drugs, contact\" prompts you to ask about which three areas?","choices":["Insect bites, antibiotic allergies, and the use of gloves at work","Bacterial cultures, prescription records, and patch testing results","Pets in the home, over-the-counter creams, and laundry detergent brand","Affected family members or contacts and travel, systemic medications"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — infectious exposure, drug exposure and contact exposure, in that order. Allergens or irritants encountered through hobbies, occupation or environment. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which conditions cause proptosis or exophthalmos?","choices":["Conjunctivitis and episcleritis","Retrobulbar haemorrhage, orbital cellulitis, orbital tumour, and Graves disease","Cataract, glaucoma and macular degeneration","Blepharitis, chalazion and hordeolum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the four orbital causes. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A patient has intact skin over the sacrum with an area of erythema that fails to blanch with pressure. What stage of pressure ulcer is this?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the skin is still intact, and the non-blanching erythema is the key finding. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What are the fundoscopic findings in papilloedema, and what causes it?","choices":["A white disc with absent tiny vessels — from death of optic nerve fibres","An enlarged cup more than half the disc diameter — from raised intraocular pressure","Cotton-wool patches and hard exudates — from diabetes","A pink swollen disc with blurred margins and no visible cup, with loss of vessel pulsations"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the findings and cause. From raised intracranial pressure. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A green discoloration of the nail suggests infection with which organism?","choices":["Candida","Pseudomonas","A dermatophyte fungus","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the pigment it produces gives the nail its green colour. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What does the pinhole test distinguish, and by what mechanism?","choices":["An afferent from an efferent pupil defect","A refractive error from other causes","Glaucoma from cataract, by measuring pressure indirectly","A field defect from an acuity problem, by isolating quadrants"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is both the purpose and the optics. The pinhole admits only light perpendicular to the lens, so the light need not be bent to focus on the retina. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which findings suggest a zygomatic fracture?","choices":["A sunken eye with diplopia on upward gaze","Proptosis with restricted painful movement","A 360-degree subconjunctival haemorrhage","Flattening of the malar eminence best seen from behind the seated patient looking down, oedema and ecchymosis of the temple or infraorbital area, and a palpable step-off on palpating the zygoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — Beck called this one important to know. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"Spoon-shaped concave nails in which the nail plate thins and becomes inverted are called what?","choices":["Nail pitting","Koilonychia","Onycholysis","Clubbing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the concavity is deep enough that the nail can hold a drop of water. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A patient with atopic dermatitis has thickened skin with accentuated skin lines from years of scratching. What is this called?","choices":["Collarette scaling","Lichenification","Keloid formation","Callus formation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a secondary lesion caused by chronic irritation. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A BLUE sclera indicates what?","choices":["Osteogenesis imperfecta","Liver disease","Thyroid eye disease","Long-term steroid use"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the association for blue sclerae. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which pair of diagnoses must be differentiated because the latter can lead to death?","choices":["Cataract from glaucoma","Hordeolum from chalazion","Preseptal from orbital cellulitis","Episcleritis from scleritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is exactly this reason. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How does fluorescein reveal a corneal defect?","choices":["The dye changes the colour of the aqueous humour","Orange dye is instilled and a blue light used","The dye stains intact epithelium and spares defects","The dye is injected and photographed with a blue flash"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — uptake marks where the epithelium is missing. The dye is taken up by areas of cornea devoid of epithelium. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Which three findings together describe the tympanic membrane in acute otitis media?","choices":["Red, retracted, and with the landmarks exaggerated","Pale, thickened and immobile","Amber with visible bubbles","Red, bulging, and with the landmarks lost"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The purulent effusion behind it pushes it outward and obscures the normal anatomy. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Eye pain WITH HEADACHE suggests what?","choices":["Conjunctivitis","Acute narrow angle glaucoma","Temporal arteritis","Optic neuritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the pairing for headache plus eye pain. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is needed to inspect the POSTERIOR pharynx properly?","choices":["A tongue blade","An otoscope with the largest speculum","A nasal speculum","Transillumination in a dark room"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The tongue obscures the posterior wall and has to be depressed out of the way. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Entropion and ectropion both affect which eyelid?","choices":["The upper lid","Both lids equally","Neither — they affect the conjunctiva","The lower lid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is the lower lid for both. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is hyperaemia?","choices":["Injection of the superficially visible vessels of the conjunctiva, episclera or sclera","Swelling of the conjunctiva","Blood collecting beneath the conjunctiva","Inward folding of the lower lid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the definition, and it names all three layers. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Lesions confined to sun-exposed skin should raise concern for which condition?","choices":["Intertrigo","Herpes zoster","Psoriasis","Skin cancers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — cumulative ultraviolet exposure explains why these arise where the sun reaches. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"When is globe rupture more likely in a patient with a subconjunctival haemorrhage?","choices":["When the haemorrhage is small and sectoral","When the patient is anticoagulated","When both eyes are affected","In trauma, and when the haemorrhage encircles the entire cornea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is both the mechanism and the 360-degree pattern. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Transverse depressions in the fingernail found halfway up the nail suggest what?","choices":["Chronic hypoxia from lung disease","Iron deficiency of long standing","An illness about three months previously","An illness within the past two weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — these are Beau's lines, and the position on the nail dates the insult. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"What distinguishes terminal hair from vellus hair?","choices":["Terminal hair is short and fine while vellus hair is coarse","Terminal hair grows only after puberty while vellus hair is present only in infancy","Terminal hair is pigmented while vellus hair is always white","Terminal hair is coarse and grows on the scalp and in the pubic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the two hair types differ in calibre as well as location. Axillary and beard areas, while vellus hair is short and fine and covers the body. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Painless separation of the nail plate from the nail bed, beginning distally, is called what?","choices":["Onycholysis","Koilonychia","Paronychia","Subungual hematoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — several or all nails are usually affected. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What usually causes a tympanic membrane perforation, and what may accompany it?","choices":["Otitis media, and drainage may come through the perforation","Otitis externa, with itching through the perforation","Barotrauma, with vertigo through the perforation","Cerumen impaction, with pain through the perforation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The perforation is usually the consequence of middle ear disease rather than a primary event. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A patient has hair loss in multiple round patches with hairs that taper toward the scalp. What is the likely diagnosis?","choices":["Androgenic alopecia","Tinea capitis","Trichotillomania","Alopecia areata"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — these exclamation point hairs, along with round patches, characterise this chronic inflammatory disease of hair follicles. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"How long does benign positional vertigo last overall, as opposed to how long a single episode lasts?","choices":["Each episode is under a minute, and the condition resolves within 24 hours","Episodes last hours, and the condition lasts a few weeks","Episodes and the condition both last a few weeks","Each episode is under a minute, but the condition lasts a few weeks and may recur"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The table separates the two timescales, and confusing them is the usual error. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the rule about topical anaesthetic in corneal abrasion?","choices":["It should never be used at all","It is only useful if the cornea is hazy","It brings immediate relief","It should be prescribed for home use to control pain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the material draws exactly this line. But should be used for DIAGNOSIS and not as a treatment. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"Which list contains only CONDUCTIVE causes of hearing loss?","choices":["Rubella, cytomegalovirus and hereditary loss","Cerumen, foreign bodies, effusions, exostoses, tumours and tympanic membrane perforation","Presbycusis, noise exposure, acoustic neuroma and Ménière disease","Cerumen, presbycusis, effusions and noise exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Every one of them obstructs or loads the external or middle ear. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How is the nasolacrimal duct obstruction test performed, and what is a positive result?","choices":["Evert the upper lid and inspect the tarsal conjunctiva","Ask the patient to look up, press on the lower lid near the medial canthus just inside the bony orbit to compress the lacrimal sac, and look for fluid regurgitating from the puncta — mucopurulent fluid suggests obstruction","Instil fluorescein and time its disappearance from the tear film","Press on the upper lid near the lateral canthus and look for tearing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the full technique and its positive finding. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which term describes a lacy or networked pattern of lesions?","choices":["Reticular","Linear","Gyrate","Arciform"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the net-like pattern gives the term its name. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Once local eye disease is excluded, which three causes of a dilated pupil remain?","choices":["Optic neuritis, papilloedema and optic atrophy","Compression or other lesion of the third cranial nerve","Glaucoma, cataract and macular degeneration","Horner syndrome, myasthenia gravis and Graves disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the three, and Beck called them important to know. Parasympathetic denervation from a ciliary ganglion lesion producing an Adie's pupil; and pharmacologic block of the pupillary sphincter. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A raised, shiny, slow-growing lesion on the auricle with visible telangiectasia. What should be suspected?","choices":["A cutaneous cyst","A keloid","A tophus","Basal cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Raised, shiny, telangiectatic and slow growing — and malignant, which is what separates it from the benign lumps in the same list. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How should subjective and objective information be arranged in a note?","choices":["Subjective information may be omitted when the examination is complete","Objective findings recorded within the assessment section","Each kept in its own appropriate section, not mixed together","Combined into a single narrative so the encounter reads chronologically"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — blending them is one of the commonest documentation errors. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 20"},{"q":"An abnormally ENLARGED head raises which two conditions?","choices":["Hypothyroidism and acromegaly","Paget disease and microcephaly","Hydrocephalus and Paget disease","Microcephaly and hydrocephalus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One expands the skull from within, the other thickens the bone itself. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Which corneal ulcer is NOT very painful?","choices":["Bacterial corneal ulcers","Fungal corneal ulcers","Amoebic corneal ulcers","Herpes simplex corneal ulcers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the material notes this exception explicitly. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which epistaxis history suggests a SYSTEMIC problem rather than a local one?","choices":["Bleeding that follows a recent upper respiratory infection","Bleeding in dry winter air","Recurrent bleeding, or bleeding and bruising elsewhere on the body","Bleeding from one nostril after digital trauma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One nosebleed is local until proven otherwise; a pattern of bleeding at other sites changes the question entirely. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which descriptive terms are used for skin moisture?","choices":["Dryness, sweating and oiliness","Roughness and smoothness","Warmth and coolness","Mobility and turgor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — these three cover the moisture characteristic. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Where does a chalazion usually point?","choices":["At the medial canthus","At the lateral canthus","Inside the lid rather than on the lid margin","On the lid margin at the lash line"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the material notes this as a distinguishing feature. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Necrotising ulcerative gingivitis is known by which other names?","choices":["Aphthous stomatitis, and canker sores","Vincent's angina, and trench mouth","Ludwig's angina, and quinsy","Angular cheilitis, and perleche"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The second name records where it was first described in numbers, in the trenches of the First World War. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A translucent, pearly nodule with a depressed centre and raised borders on the face is characteristic of which malignancy?","choices":["Squamous cell carcinoma","Malignant melanoma","Kaposi's sarcoma","Basal cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it may ulcerate, and a non-healing ulcer on the face should raise the same suspicion. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How is the RINNE test performed, in order?","choices":["Fork on the mastoid until the sound stops","Fork close to the canal first, then on the mastoid once the sound stops","Fork on the vertex of the head, then moved to the mastoid","Fork held in front of both ears simultaneously"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bone conduction is measured first and air conduction second, and the comparison is what the test reports. The mnemonic taught was Rinne under the pinna. Then moved close to the canal and the patient asked if it is still heard. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which term describes lesions clustered in a dermatomal distribution?","choices":["Herpetiform","Serpiginous","Iris","Zosteriform"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — named for the pattern seen in herpes zoster. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A lesion with a central red macule and radiating spider-like arms that blanches is called what?","choices":["A spider angioma","A cherry angioma","A telangiectasia","Purpura"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the radiating arms and blanching together identify it. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which finding is described by the term rubor?","choices":["Loss of normal skin colour","A bluish discoloration of the skin","Erythema, or redness of the skin","Yellowing of the skin and sclerae"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — rubor and erythema are used interchangeably for redness. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How does a septal haematoma form?","choices":["Blood collects within the cartilage itself","Chronic inflammation erodes the septum until it bleeds","Venous engorgement of the turbinates spreads to the septum","Injury disrupts the vessels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The separation is the problem, not the volume of blood. Pulling the lining away from the cartilage so blood collects between the two. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which peripheral vertigo has sudden onset lasting hours to two weeks, with hearing unaffected and tinnitus absent?","choices":["Benign positional vertigo","Drug toxicity","Vestibular neuronitis","Ménière disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it may recur over twelve to eighteen months. Nausea, vomiting and nystagmus accompany it. Also called acute labyrinthitis. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A collection of cellular debris, dried serum and blood forming a scab is called what?","choices":["A crust","A scale","An erosion","A fissure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the antecedent primary lesion is usually a vesicle, bulla or pustule. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Hard nodules on the auricle made of uric acid crystals. What are they, and what do they indicate?","choices":["Cutaneous cysts, indicating follicular obstruction","Exostoses, indicating repeated cold water exposure","Tophi, indicating chronic tophaceous gout","Keloids, indicating previous trauma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They are deposits rather than growths, and finding them on the ear points to long-standing disease. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A thin flake of exfoliated epidermis, as seen in dandruff, is called what?","choices":["A plaque","Lichenification","A scale","A crust"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — cradle cap is the other example given. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is a NORMAL Weber result?","choices":["Midline, or heard equally in both ears","Lateralised to the right","Heard only through bone conduction","Not heard at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. There is no lateralisation to report when both sides transmit equally. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which headache history suggests a MASS?","choices":["Episodic and unilateral","Bilateral and band-like","New, progressive and persistent","Sudden and severe"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All three together — a headache that is new to the patient, is getting worse, and does not remit. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"In unilateral SENSORINEURAL loss, which way does the Weber sound lateralise, and why?","choices":["To the GOOD ear","To the IMPAIRED ear, because the damage amplifies bone conduction","It does not lateralise in sensorineural loss","It lateralises to whichever ear was tested first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The damaged side cannot process the tone however it arrives, so the sound is heard where the machinery still works. Because inner ear or cochlear nerve damage impairs transmission on the affected side. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Eye pain WITH BLINKING suggests what?","choices":["Temporal arteritis","Corneal abrasion or a foreign body","Acute narrow angle glaucoma","Optic neuritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the lid moving over the defect is what hurts. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How should communication style be handled across different patients?","choices":["Keep communication strictly to the presenting clinical question","Adapt the style and content of communication appropriately for each patient","Use consistent phrasing with every patient so nothing is missed","Match the register of whoever referred the patient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — communication is expected to change to suit the patient rather than following one fixed script. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 23"},{"q":"Insidious vertigo with hearing impaired on ONE side, tinnitus present, and possible cranial nerve V and VII involvement. Which is it?","choices":["Acoustic neuroma compressing the vestibular branch of cranial nerve VIII","Ménière disease","Central vertigo from a brainstem lesion","Drug toxicity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The unilateral hearing loss plus the neighbouring cranial nerves is the signature — the tumour sits where V and VII run close by. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why must the upper lid be everted in a corneal abrasion?","choices":["To apply fluorescein more evenly","To assess the tear film","A foreign body embedded in the upper tarsal conjunctiva can repeatedly scratch the cornea as the eye opens and closes","To measure the intraocular pressure accurately"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the reason, which is why the abrasion recurs otherwise. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 120"},{"q":"What accompanies a sunken eye in an orbital floor fracture, and how is it best observed?","choices":["Photophobia and a dendrite — best seen with fluorescein","A red painless patch — best seen in ambient light","Ecchymosis, point tenderness and a palpable step-off around the orbital rim — best observed from above the head looking down toward the eyes","Fever and purulent discharge — best observed with a slit lamp"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the associated findings and its observation technique. — PD II Advanced Exam Ocular Lecture - Beck.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Painful haemorrhagic vesicles on the tympanic membrane or canal. What is this, and what causes it?","choices":["Tympanosclerosis with haemorrhage","Bullous myringitis","Bullous myringitis, which is always viral","Otitis externa with secondary infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The vesicles are the finding, and the cause is not settled by the appearance. Which may be viral or bacterial. — PD II ENT 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which gland secretes sweat in order to maintain body temperature?","choices":["The sudoriferous (eccrine) gland","The apocrine gland","The sebaceous gland","The ceruminous gland"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — thermoregulatory sweating is the eccrine gland's job. — PD II Derm - Beck.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why can reusing your own earlier written work create an academic integrity problem?","choices":["Reused documents often carry formatting errors","Faculty cannot grade the same document twice","Work is not original when it has already been submitted for academic credit","Earlier work may contain clinical guidance that has since changed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which is why reusing a template from a previous assignment carries a warning. Whether by you or anyone else. — Intro to PD II - Elwaya .pdf, Page 22"}]};
